---
<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="70–80 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <a class="back" href="/curriculum/#lvl-B2">Back to curriculum</a>
  <p class="overline">B2 Vocabulary · Degree, scale, collocation, stance, and evidence</p>
  <h1>Nuanced adjectives &amp; intensifiers</h1>
  <p class="lede">A stronger word is not automatically a more precise word. Decide what was observed, where it belongs on a scale, and how certain the evaluation should be before turning up the volume.</p>
  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can distinguish a measurable fact from an evaluation; identify gradable, maximum or endpoint, extreme, classifying, and participial adjective readings without treating the labels as permanent boxes; choose limited, moderate, high, near-endpoint, and complete degree; use established combinations with <span class="ex">highly, deeply, strongly, bitterly, perfectly, entirely, absolutely</span>, and <span class="ex">utterly</span>; control <span class="ex">too, enough, so, such, almost, nearly, not quite</span>, and comparative modifiers; explain how <span class="ex">quite, pretty, fairly, somewhat, rather</span>, and <span class="ex">really</span> affect stance and register; avoid converting limited evidence into an extreme claim; hear U.S. thought groups and contrastive prominence; and deliver a balanced recommendation whose adjectives, intensifiers, criteria, and evidence all agree.</p>

  <h2>Retrieve the language this lesson depends on</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You recovered comparative degree, collocation, evidence calibration, register, and the difference between fact and evaluation.">
    <h3>Five prerequisite decisions</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Nuanced adjective prerequisite choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="considerably more practical">considerably more practical</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="strong evidence">strong evidence</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a qualified conclusion">a qualified conclusion</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="neutral-professional wording">neutral-professional wording</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an evaluation against a purpose">an evaluation against a purpose</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the most dramatic adjective available">the most dramatic adjective available</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Version B reduces six steps to three, while Version A reduces six to five. Version B is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="considerably more practical" data-hint="Use a modifier that can intensify a comparative, not very before more." data-fix="Version B is considerably more practical than Version A.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>English conventionally combines the adjective and noun in <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="strong evidence" data-hint="Choose the established adjective-noun partnership rather than a literal measure of muscle." data-fix="Strong evidence is an established collocation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>When a small voluntary sample supports only a limited inference, draw <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a qualified conclusion" data-hint="Match claim scope and certainty to the available evidence." data-fix="A small voluntary sample supports a qualified conclusion.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>A public report normally replaces <span class="ex">totally awesome</span> with <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="neutral-professional wording" data-hint="Choose a register that makes the criterion and evidence visible." data-fix="A public report normally uses neutral-professional wording.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span><span class="ex">The report has 300 pages</span> is a count. <span class="ex">It is too long for a ten-minute briefing</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an evaluation against a purpose" data-hint="Too identifies a degree that exceeds a limit for a named use." data-fix="Too long evaluates length against the ten-minute purpose.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice: the same pilot can support different evaluations</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Marston Library quiet-pod review</h3>
    <p><b>Available summary:</b> The library tested two quiet pods for six weeks. Forty-eight of 52 bookable sessions were used. Eighty-three people used a pod, and 57 answered an optional survey. Of those respondents, 41 said the pod helped them focus. Sound readings averaged seven decibels lower inside a pod than in the surrounding room. Four booked sessions ended early after users reported that the pods felt uncomfortably warm.</p>
    <p><b>Reviewer A:</b> The pods appear moderately useful, but the evidence is fairly limited.</p>
    <p><b>Reviewer B:</b> The pods are absolutely transformative and completely successful.</p>
    <p><b>Reviewer C:</b> The measured noise reduction is substantial. The satisfaction result is promising but not conclusive, and the ventilation complaints are a significant practical concern.</p>
  </div>
  <p>Reviewer B uses stronger language, but the summary does not support every part of that evaluation. The pilot measured sound and collected an optional survey. It did not compare learning, work quality, or long-term use, so <span class="ex">transformative</span> and <span class="ex">completely successful</span> exceed the evidence.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated measured sound, optional survey evidence, moderate evaluation, unsupported total success, a bounded positive adjective, and a criterion-specific concern.">
    <h3>Discovery check: locate evidence before degree</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Nuanced adjective discovery choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an average seven-decibel difference">an average seven-decibel difference</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="41 of 57 optional survey respondents">41 of 57 optional survey respondents</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a positive but limited judgment">a positive but limited judgment</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a claim of total success that the summary does not establish">a claim of total success that the summary does not establish</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="strong enough to matter without claiming an endpoint">strong enough to matter without claiming an endpoint</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="four early endings linked to uncomfortable warmth">four early endings linked to uncomfortable warmth</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="proof of permanent improvement in every user's work">proof of permanent improvement in every user's work</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The directly measured noise result is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an average seven-decibel difference" data-hint="Choose the instrument-based quantity rather than a judgment about productivity." data-fix="Sound readings averaged seven decibels lower inside the pods.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The satisfaction statement comes from <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="41 of 57 optional survey respondents" data-hint="Preserve both the positive-response count and the self-selected respondent group." data-fix="Forty-one of 57 optional survey respondents reported better focus.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span><span class="ex">Moderately useful</span> presents <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a positive but limited judgment" data-hint="Moderately places usefulness above weak but below an extreme endpoint." data-fix="Moderately useful is a positive but limited judgment.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span><span class="ex">Completely successful</span> makes <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a claim of total success that the summary does not establish" data-hint="The record includes ventilation complaints and leaves important outcomes unmeasured." data-fix="The summary does not establish complete success.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>In Reviewer C's wording, <span class="ex">substantial</span> presents the measured difference as <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="strong enough to matter without claiming an endpoint" data-hint="Choose a high but gradable evaluation rather than total transformation." data-fix="Substantial presents a meaningful degree without claiming an endpoint.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The practical basis for <span class="ex">significant concern</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="four early endings linked to uncomfortable warmth" data-hint="Tie the adjective to the reported user consequence." data-fix="Four sessions ended early after users reported uncomfortable warmth.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: classify the current reading, not the word forever</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Adjective labels describe how a word behaves in a particular meaning and context. They are useful tendencies, not permanent boxes. A speaker can extend, compare, or reinterpret a category for humor, approximation, or typicality. Start with the intended reading, then choose a conventional degree pattern.</p>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Reading</th><th>Core question</th><th>Examples</th><th>Common degree resources</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>gradable quality</td><td>How much of the quality?</td><td>useful, warm, convincing, expensive</td><td>slightly, fairly, very, extremely; more or less</td></tr>
    <tr><td>maximum or endpoint</td><td>How close to the limit?</td><td>complete, empty, impossible, unanimous</td><td>almost, nearly, virtually, absolutely, completely</td></tr>
    <tr><td>lexically extreme</td><td>Does the adjective already express a high degree?</td><td>exhausted, boiling, enormous, furious</td><td>absolutely, completely, utterly; really in conversation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>classifying</td><td>Which type or category?</td><td>medical, wooden, weekly, digital</td><td>normally no degree modifier in the literal category reading</td></tr>
    <tr><td>participial or evaluative collocation</td><td>Which conventional partner expresses degree?</td><td>concerned, qualified, opposed, recommended</td><td>deeply concerned, highly qualified, strongly opposed or recommended</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <div class="note"><h3>Scale type can change with meaning</h3><p><span class="ex">The bottle is empty</span> uses an endpoint meaning, but conversational <span class="ex">almost empty</span> measures distance from that endpoint. <span class="ex">A weekly meeting</span> classifies frequency, while marked <span class="ex">That style feels very 1990s</span> means highly characteristic of an era, not more literally inside the 1990s. Explain the shifted reading instead of declaring that a word can never be graded.</p></div>
  <div class="note"><h3>Pairings are preferences, not sealed grammar gates</h3><p><span class="ex">Very tired</span> and <span class="ex">absolutely exhausted</span> are useful teaching anchors, but fluent English also allows creative and established combinations beyond a two-column chart. <span class="ex">Utterly charming</span>, for example, uses <span class="ex">utterly</span> for an extreme degree even though <span class="ex">charming</span> can otherwise be graded. Check meaning, collocation, genre, and actual usage together.</p></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You identified a gradable quality, extreme lexical choice, endpoint approach, literal category, shifted typicality reading, comparative form, comparative modifier, and near-impossibility without forcing permanent adjective boxes.">
    <h3>Choose eight scale-and-reading descriptions</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Adjective scale choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="slightly warmer">slightly warmer</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="absolutely exhausted">absolutely exhausted</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="nearly complete">nearly complete</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a weekly meeting">a weekly meeting</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="highly characteristic of 1990s style">highly characteristic of 1990s style</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="more useful">more useful</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="considerably more useful">considerably more useful</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="virtually impossible">virtually impossible</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="very more useful">very more useful</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The second room is 72°F, only two degrees above the first. It is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="slightly warmer" data-hint="Use a small-degree modifier with the comparative adjective." data-fix="The second room is slightly warmer.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>After working through the night, Ana could barely stay awake. She was <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="absolutely exhausted" data-hint="Choose the high-degree adjective and an established emphatic partner." data-fix="Ana was absolutely exhausted.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Forty-nine of 50 required records are ready, so the set is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="nearly complete" data-hint="Measure distance from the complete endpoint without claiming it has been reached." data-fix="The set is nearly complete.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>A meeting held once every week is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a weekly meeting" data-hint="Use the literal frequency category without a degree adverb." data-fix="It is a weekly meeting.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>In <span class="ex">The graphics look very 1990s</span>, <span class="ex">very</span> means <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="highly characteristic of 1990s style" data-hint="The decade label has shifted from calendar category to typical resemblance." data-fix="Very 1990s means highly characteristic of 1990s style.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>To compare two ordinary tools, say the updated tool is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="more useful" data-hint="Use the comparative form of the gradable adjective." data-fix="The updated tool is more useful.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>If testing shows a large comparative difference, the updated tool may be <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="considerably more useful" data-hint="Modify more useful with a conventional comparative intensifier, not very." data-fix="The updated tool is considerably more useful.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>The deadline leaves only one minute for a four-hour process. Completion is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="virtually impossible" data-hint="Choose a phrase meaning extremely close to the impossible endpoint, while leaving a logical sliver open." data-fix="Completion is virtually impossible in one minute.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Store adjective and intensifier as a meaningful partnership</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>An intensifier may be grammatically possible but still unusual with a particular adjective. Learn recurrent chunks and the meanings they favor. <span class="ex">Highly</span> often evaluates status, likelihood, skill, effectiveness, or significance. <span class="ex">Deeply</span> often presents an emotion, belief, involvement, or division as profound. <span class="ex">Strongly</span> commonly modifies positions, recommendations, and reactions. The pattern is semantic and collocational, not a simple synonym exchange.</p>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Reliable examples</th><th>Meaning focus</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>highly</td><td>highly effective, likely, qualified, significant, controversial</td><td>high degree on an evaluative or probability scale</td></tr>
    <tr><td>deeply</td><td>deeply concerned, disappointed, committed, divided, rooted</td><td>profound emotion, condition, or involvement</td></tr>
    <tr><td>strongly</td><td>strongly opposed, recommended, suggestive, influenced</td><td>force of position, recommendation, evidence, or effect</td></tr>
    <tr><td>bitterly</td><td>bitterly disappointed, divided, opposed, cold</td><td>painful emotion, conflict, or severe cold</td></tr>
    <tr><td>perfectly</td><td>perfectly reasonable, clear, normal, capable</td><td>complete fit with a norm or judgment</td></tr>
    <tr><td>entirely</td><td>entirely possible, appropriate, different, responsible</td><td>whole extent, often in measured professional evaluation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>utterly</td><td>utterly unacceptable, confused, wrong, dependent</td><td>absolute or extreme degree, often strongly evaluative</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You recovered ten established pairings for effectiveness, probability, concern, division, opposition, recommendation, disappointment, reasonableness, appropriateness, and rejection.">
    <h3>Complete ten collocational choices</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Adjective intensifier collocations"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="highly effective">highly effective</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="highly unlikely">highly unlikely</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="deeply concerned">deeply concerned</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="deeply divided">deeply divided</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="strongly opposed">strongly opposed</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="strongly recommended">strongly recommended</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="bitterly disappointed">bitterly disappointed</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="perfectly reasonable">perfectly reasonable</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="entirely appropriate">entirely appropriate</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="utterly unacceptable">utterly unacceptable</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="deeply effective">deeply effective</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>A controlled comparison shows the treatment consistently produces the intended result. The report calls it <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="highly effective" data-hint="Use the established high-degree partnership for effectiveness." data-fix="The treatment is highly effective in the tested conditions.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The model assigns the event a probability below 2%, so the event is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="highly unlikely" data-hint="Choose the conventional high-degree probability combination." data-fix="The event is highly unlikely under the model.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Residents express serious sustained worry about water quality. They are <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="deeply concerned" data-hint="Use the conventional profound-emotion partnership." data-fix="Residents are deeply concerned about water quality.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The committee splits evenly into two firmly opposed groups. It is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="deeply divided" data-hint="Choose the established phrase for a profound internal division." data-fix="The committee is deeply divided.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The union formally rejects the proposal with substantial force. It is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="strongly opposed" data-hint="Use the collocation that expresses force of position." data-fix="The union is strongly opposed to the proposal.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>After three successful safety trials, the review gives emphatic professional support. The change is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="strongly recommended" data-hint="Choose the conventional phrase for a forceful recommendation." data-fix="The change is strongly recommended after the safety trials.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>Mina had prepared for months and felt painfully let down when the event was canceled. She was <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="bitterly disappointed" data-hint="Choose the established partnership for painful disappointment." data-fix="Mina was bitterly disappointed.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>Given the stated deadline, asking for one additional day is fully sensible. The request is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="perfectly reasonable" data-hint="Use the collocation meaning completely within reasonable limits." data-fix="The request is perfectly reasonable.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">9</span><span>The response follows every stated professional and ethical requirement. It is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="entirely appropriate" data-hint="Choose the measured whole-extent evaluation." data-fix="The response is entirely appropriate.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">10</span><span>A proposal knowingly removes the only emergency exit. The safety officer calls it <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="utterly unacceptable" data-hint="Choose the absolute negative evaluation justified by the stated safety violation." data-fix="Removing the only emergency exit is utterly unacceptable.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Build the grammar of limits, sufficiency, result, and comparison</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>too + adjective + to-infinitive</td><td>degree exceeds a limit for the result</td><td>The sample is too small to support that claim.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>adjective + enough + to-infinitive</td><td>degree meets a limit for the result</td><td>The sample is large enough to test the procedure.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>so + adjective + that-clause</td><td>degree leads to a stated result</td><td>The label was so unclear that 18 users chose the wrong form.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>such + a/an + adjective + noun + that</td><td>noun phrase has a degree-producing quality</td><td>It was such an unclear label that 18 users chose the wrong form.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>much / far / considerably + comparative</td><td>large comparative difference</td><td>The revision is considerably clearer.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>slightly / a little + comparative</td><td>small comparative difference</td><td>The revision is slightly shorter.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>almost / nearly / virtually + endpoint</td><td>approach without necessarily reaching the limit</td><td>The checklist is almost complete.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>not quite / not entirely + adjective</td><td>partial shortfall or diplomatic qualification</td><td>The explanation is not entirely clear.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <div class="note"><h3>Too does not simply mean very</h3><p><span class="ex">Very expensive</span> reports a high degree. <span class="ex">Too expensive for the current budget</span> says the price exceeds a relevant limit. Name the purpose, person, standard, or consequence when the limit would otherwise be unclear. <span class="ex">Enough</span> follows an adjective but precedes a noun: <span class="ex">clear enough</span>, <span class="ex">enough evidence</span>.</p></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You formed excess, sufficiency, so-result, such-noun result, large and small comparison, near-endpoint, and diplomatic partial criticism with explicit consequences.">
    <h3>Complete eight degree-grammar decisions</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Degree grammar choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="too small to support a general claim">too small to support a general claim</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="large enough to test the procedure">large enough to test the procedure</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="so limited that the result cannot be generalized">so limited that the result cannot be generalized</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="such a limited sample that the result cannot be generalized">such a limited sample that the result cannot be generalized</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="much more reliable than the original">much more reliable than the original</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="slightly less expensive than Option A">slightly less expensive than Option A</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="nowhere near complete">nowhere near complete</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="not entirely clear">not entirely clear</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="enough clear for everybody">enough clear for everybody</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Twelve volunteers cannot represent a national workforce. The sample is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="too small to support a general claim" data-hint="Use too plus adjective plus consequence to identify the exceeded evidence limit." data-fix="The sample is too small to support a general claim.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Thirty trial accounts meet the technical team's minimum of 25 for a procedure test. The sample is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="large enough to test the procedure" data-hint="Place enough after the adjective and name the supported purpose." data-fix="The sample is large enough to test the procedure.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Complete the adjective-result frame: The evidence is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="so limited that the result cannot be generalized" data-hint="Use so before the adjective and that before the resulting clause." data-fix="The evidence is so limited that the result cannot be generalized.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Complete the noun-phrase result frame: It is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="such a limited sample that the result cannot be generalized" data-hint="Use such a before adjective plus singular count noun." data-fix="It is such a limited sample that the result cannot be generalized.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The revised sensor succeeds in 96% of trials, compared with 71% for the original. It is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="much more reliable than the original" data-hint="Use a large-difference modifier before the comparative." data-fix="The revised sensor is much more reliable than the original.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Option B costs $980, compared with $1,000 for Option A. Option B is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="slightly less expensive than Option A" data-hint="Use a small-difference modifier before less expensive." data-fix="Option B is slightly less expensive than Option A.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>Only 12 of 50 required entries are present. The record is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="nowhere near complete" data-hint="Choose the phrase showing a large distance from the endpoint." data-fix="The record is nowhere near complete.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>A reviewer understands the main point but cannot identify the proposed deadline. A diplomatic accurate evaluation is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="not entirely clear" data-hint="Choose partial criticism rather than total incomprehensibility." data-fix="The proposal is not entirely clear about the deadline.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Choose stance and register, not a hidden percentage</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Degree adverbs also signal relationship and voice. In U.S. English, <span class="ex">pretty useful</span> is common and conversational, <span class="ex">fairly useful</span> is more neutral, and <span class="ex">somewhat useful</span> often sounds measured or written. <span class="ex">Rather</span> can sound more marked and may suggest that the degree is notable, unexpected, or mildly critical. <span class="ex">Really</span> is flexible and common in conversation.</p>
  <div class="note"><h3>Quite has more than one degree meaning</h3><p><span class="ex">Quite</span> can mean completely, extremely, or to a considerable extent. In the U.S. target, <span class="ex">quite good</span> may mean considerably good, while <span class="ex">quite impossible</span> presents complete impossibility and <span class="ex">not quite ready</span> means short of the endpoint. Adjective, syntax, intonation, region, and context matter. Do not assign one fixed percentage.</p></div>
  <div class="note"><h3>Intensifiers do not create evidence</h3><p><span class="ex">Highly significant</span> does not prove statistical significance unless the analysis reports an appropriate statistical result. <span class="ex">Absolutely safe</span> is rarely defensible from a limited pilot. In professional evaluation, name the criterion and evidence: <span class="ex">highly effective at reducing duplicate messages in the 300-case staging test</span>.</p></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched conversational, neutral, measured-written, marked, complete, and evidence-calibrated stances to six explicit contexts.">
    <h3>Complete six stance and register choices</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Degree stance choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="pretty useful for quick team notes">pretty useful for quick team notes</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="fairly useful in the tested workflow">fairly useful in the tested workflow</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="somewhat limited by the voluntary sample">somewhat limited by the voluntary sample</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="rather surprising given the earlier failures">rather surprising given the earlier failures</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="quite impossible under the stated rule">quite impossible under the stated rule</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="promising but not conclusive">promising but not conclusive</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="absolutely proven for every possible user">absolutely proven for every possible user</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>In a familiar team chat, a tool saves a little time on informal notes. A natural conversational evaluation is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="pretty useful for quick team notes" data-hint="Choose the common conversational modifier with a bounded purpose." data-fix="The tool is pretty useful for quick team notes.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>A neutral internal report sees a moderate benefit only in the tested process. It calls the tool <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="fairly useful in the tested workflow" data-hint="Choose a moderate neutral evaluation whose scope remains explicit." data-fix="The tool is fairly useful in the tested workflow.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>A written review qualifies a result because participants chose whether to answer. The evidence is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="somewhat limited by the voluntary sample" data-hint="Choose the measured written downtoner and name the limitation." data-fix="The evidence is somewhat limited by the voluntary sample.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The new version succeeds after five earlier versions failed completely. The success is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="rather surprising given the earlier failures" data-hint="Choose the marked modifier that can present a notable or unexpected degree." data-fix="The success is rather surprising given the earlier failures.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>A rule requires two signatures, and the document has none. Approval is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="quite impossible under the stated rule" data-hint="Here quite presents the complete endpoint meaning of impossible." data-fix="Approval is quite impossible under the stated two-signature rule.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>A small pilot shows a favorable pattern but has no comparison group. Call the result <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="promising but not conclusive" data-hint="Preserve positive direction and the evidence boundary together." data-fix="The result is promising but not conclusive.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Pronunciation focus: make the degree contrast audible in U.S. speech</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>In a neutral description, the adjective often carries the main prominence: <span class="ex">highly efFECtive</span>, <span class="ex">deeply conCERNED</span>. When the degree itself corrects a previous claim, prominence moves to the modifier: <span class="ex">not FAIRly effective | HIGHly effective</span>. In comparative phrases, keep the comparative relationship together: <span class="ex">CONsiderably more REliable</span>.</p>
  <p>Downtoners are often lighter than the evaluation they modify: <span class="ex">slightly unCLEAR</span>. A consequence may form a new thought group: <span class="ex">too SMALL | to represent the POPulation</span>. Do not stress every intensifier automatically. Prominence follows the information contrast, and a dramatic delivery can make a measured adjective sound stronger than the evidence warrants.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read default adjective prominence, corrected intensifier prominence, comparative chunking, too-result thought groups, and qualified not-entirely delivery" data-success-message="Correct. You heard default adjective focus, corrected degree, comparative chunking, limit-and-result grouping, and partial qualification.">
    <h3>Five spoken-degree decisions</h3>
    <details><summary>Tutor script and delivery notes</summary><ol><li>The procedure was highly efFECtive in the tested setting.</li><li>Not FAIRly effective | HIGHly effective.</li><li>The revised sensor is CONsiderably more reLIable.</li><li>The sample is too SMALL | to represent the POPulation.</li><li>The deadline is not entirely CLEAR.</li></ol><p>Read each line once neutrally and once with the stated contrast. Keep unstressed degree words light unless they correct the previous degree. Preserve the evidence scope even when the voice becomes emphatic.</p></details>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Spoken adjective choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the effectiveness evaluation as new information">the effectiveness evaluation as new information</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a correction from moderate to high degree">a correction from moderate to high degree</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="one comparative relationship with a large difference">one comparative relationship with a large difference</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the exceeded limit and its consequence">the exceeded limit and its consequence</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="partial lack of clarity rather than total incomprehensibility">partial lack of clarity rather than total incomprehensibility</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a rule that every intensifier must receive maximum stress">a rule that every intensifier must receive maximum stress</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span><span class="contraction">Tutor line 1:</span> Main prominence on <span class="ex">effective</span> presents <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the effectiveness evaluation as new information" data-hint="No previous degree is being corrected, so the lexical evaluation carries the focus." data-fix="Effective carries the new evaluation in the neutral line.">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span><span class="contraction">Tutor line 2:</span> Prominence on <span class="ex">fairly</span> and <span class="ex">highly</span> marks <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a correction from moderate to high degree" data-hint="The speaker rejects one degree and supplies a stronger one." data-fix="The contrast corrects moderate fairly to high highly.">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span><span class="contraction">Tutor line 3:</span> <span class="ex">Considerably more reliable</span> expresses <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="one comparative relationship with a large difference" data-hint="Keep degree modifier, comparative marker, and adjective inside one relationship." data-fix="The phrase presents one large comparative difference.">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span><span class="contraction">Tutor line 4:</span> The pause separates <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the exceeded limit and its consequence" data-hint="Too small names the limit failure; the following infinitive names what it prevents." data-fix="The line groups the exceeded sample-size limit and its consequence.">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span><span class="contraction">Tutor line 5:</span> <span class="ex">Not entirely clear</span> communicates <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="partial lack of clarity rather than total incomprehensibility" data-hint="The qualifier leaves some clarity intact." data-fix="Not entirely clear presents partial lack of clarity.">listen</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Read the complete Marston evidence record</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="reading-text">
    <p><b>Design and use:</b> Marston Library installed one quiet pod in each of two branches for a six-week pilot from March 4 through April 14. The pods offered 52 bookable 45-minute sessions; 48 sessions were used by 83 distinct users. The project cost $14,800 to install and would cost $120 per month to maintain.</p>
    <p><b>Measured sound:</b> Staff recorded sound during 20 matched periods. Sound levels averaged 51 decibels in the surrounding rooms and 44 decibels inside the pods. The record does not report a statistical test, speech-intelligibility measure, or comparison with another quiet-room design.</p>
    <p><b>Optional survey:</b> Fifty-seven of the 83 users answered an optional survey. Forty-one respondents said the pod made it easier to focus, nine reported no difference, and seven said it made focus harder. The pilot did not measure task accuracy, completed work, learning, or long-term use.</p>
    <p><b>Ventilation and decision:</b> Four of the 52 booked sessions ended early after users reported uncomfortable warmth. Staff did not record temperature inside the pods. The review recommends testing improved ventilation for another eight weeks before buying more pods. It describes the concept as promising, not proven.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You reconstructed use, sound difference, missing methods, optional survey scope, response distribution, unmeasured outcomes, ventilation evidence, and the calibrated next step."><h3>Eight evidence-and-degree decisions</h3><div class="quiz-list">
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Forty-eight of 52 sessions were used by 83 distinct users during the six-week pilot." data-hint="Preserve booked-session use, distinct-user count, and duration." data-fix="The pilot used 48 of 52 sessions across 83 users in six weeks."><p class="quiz-question">1. Which sentence accurately describes use without evaluating success?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Forty-eight of 52 sessions were used by 83 distinct users during the six-week pilot.">Forty-eight of 52 sessions were used by 83 distinct users during the six-week pilot.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Every possible session was used by exactly 52 people for six months.">Every possible session was used by exactly 52 people for six months.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="No one used either pod during the pilot.">No one used either pod during the pilot.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Average recorded sound was seven decibels lower inside the pods during 20 matched periods." data-hint="Subtract 44 from 51 and preserve the matched-period scope." data-fix="The measured average difference was seven decibels across 20 matched periods."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which measured finding is directly supported?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Average recorded sound was seven decibels lower inside the pods during 20 matched periods.">Average recorded sound was seven decibels lower inside the pods during 20 matched periods.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The pods eliminated every sound in both branches.">The pods eliminated every sound in both branches.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Every user's work quality improved by seven percent.">Every user's work quality improved by seven percent.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The record reports no statistical test, speech-intelligibility measure, or alternative quiet-room comparison." data-hint="Identify the three stated limits in the sound-method paragraph." data-fix="Those three methods or comparisons are not reported."><p class="quiz-question">3. Which limitation applies to the sound evidence?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The record reports no statistical test, speech-intelligibility measure, or alternative quiet-room comparison.">The record reports no statistical test, speech-intelligibility measure, or alternative quiet-room comparison.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The sound record includes a ten-year randomized comparison with every room design.">The sound record includes a ten-year randomized comparison with every room design.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="No sound readings were collected anywhere.">No sound readings were collected anywhere.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Fifty-seven of 83 users chose to answer, so the responses describe respondents rather than every user automatically." data-hint="Keep the response fraction and voluntary nature visible." data-fix="The optional survey supports claims about respondents, with caution about all users."><p class="quiz-question">4. Why should the survey result be qualified?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Fifty-seven of 83 users chose to answer, so the responses describe respondents rather than every user automatically.">Fifty-seven of 83 users chose to answer, so the responses describe respondents rather than every user automatically.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="All 83 users were required to report exactly the same opinion.">All 83 users were required to report exactly the same opinion.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The survey measured only room temperature with no opinion question.">The survey measured only room temperature with no opinion question.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Among respondents, 41 reported easier focus, nine no difference, and seven harder focus." data-hint="Preserve all three response categories rather than reporting only the favorable group." data-fix="The full response distribution is 41, nine, and seven."><p class="quiz-question">5. Which sentence represents the complete survey distribution?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Among respondents, 41 reported easier focus, nine no difference, and seven harder focus.">Among respondents, 41 reported easier focus, nine no difference, and seven harder focus.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="All 57 respondents reported perfect focus.">All 57 respondents reported perfect focus.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Forty-one users reported that the library had become completely silent.">Forty-one users reported that the library had become completely silent.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The pilot did not measure task accuracy, completed work, learning, or long-term use." data-hint="Do not convert self-reported ease of focus into an unmeasured performance outcome." data-fix="Those four outcomes remain unmeasured."><p class="quiz-question">6. Which claim remains outside the evidence?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The pilot did not measure task accuracy, completed work, learning, or long-term use.">The pilot did not measure task accuracy, completed work, learning, or long-term use.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The pilot measured task accuracy every day for five years.">The pilot measured task accuracy every day for five years.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The survey directly proves permanent learning gains for every user.">The survey directly proves permanent learning gains for every user.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Four sessions ended early after warmth complaints, but no internal temperature was recorded." data-hint="Keep the reported consequence and the missing measurement together." data-fix="Four early endings support a practical concern, while temperature degree remains unmeasured."><p class="quiz-question">7. Which ventilation evaluation is calibrated?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Four sessions ended early after warmth complaints, but no internal temperature was recorded.">Four sessions ended early after warmth complaints, but no internal temperature was recorded.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The pods were proven dangerously hot in every session by complete temperature records.">The pods were proven dangerously hot in every session by complete temperature records.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="No participant ever mentioned warmth.">No participant ever mentioned warmth.</button></div></div>
