  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="enough clear" data-hint="Enough follows an adjective but precedes a noun." data-fix="The instructions are clear enough for a first-time user." data-why="Adjective plus enough expresses sufficient degree. Compare clear enough with enough detail, where enough precedes the noun." data-success-message="Correct. Enough now follows the adjective and the sentence names the relevant user."><h3>Repair 4: enough position</h3><p>Tap the phrase with reversed adjective and degree-word order.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="instructions">The instructions</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="are">are</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="enough clear">enough clear</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="user">for a first-time user.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p></div>

  <h2>Rehearse evidence-first evaluation aloud</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-transform><p class="note-line">State the observation first. Then choose an adjective, degree, collocation, criterion, and evidence boundary before revealing the model.</p><div class="transform-list">
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>1</b> Report a seven-decibel average difference without claiming silence.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>The measured noise reduction was substantial, although the pods were not completely silent.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>2</b> Evaluate 41 positive responses among 57 optional survey respondents.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>The survey result is promising but somewhat limited by voluntary response.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>3</b> Explain that four warmth complaints deserve attention without claiming measured danger.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>The four early endings are a significant practical concern, but no internal temperature was recorded.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>4</b> Use too to connect sample size with an unsupported conclusion.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>The voluntary sample is too limited to support a claim about every user.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>5</b> Compare a revised design with the original after several practical improvements.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>The revised design is considerably more practical than the original.</strong></p></div>
    <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>6</b> Replace an unsupported absolute recommendation with the B2 assessment style.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>The concept is promising but not conclusive; an eight-week ventilation trial is a reasonable next step.</strong></p></div>
  </div></div>

  <h2>Practice evaluation without private disclosure</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>You never need to evaluate your own body, health, intelligence, accent, income, job performance, relationship, immigration history, legal matter, family member, trauma, confidential workplace result, or another person's identity. Use Marston Library, public product information, or a tutor-created fictional proposal. The skill is aligning language degree with criteria and evidence, not judging a person.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Scale map:</b> Place six fictional outcomes from limited through moderate, substantial, very high, near-complete, and complete. Explain the criterion for every position.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Collocation editor:</b> Replace five uses of very with established chunks, then restore very wherever the replacement changes the intended meaning or sounds inflated.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Evidence brake:</b> Hear an extreme evaluation, ask what was measured, and reduce the adjective or narrow its scope until the evidence supports it.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Register shift:</b> Evaluate one result in familiar chat, a neutral report, and an academic recommendation. Preserve the evidence while changing stance.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Prominence repair:</b> Correct adjective, degree, comparative target, or consequence aloud by moving the main prominence to the disputed information.</div>

  <h2>Final production and check</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card"><h3>Final production: lead a two-minute precision review</h3><p>Use Marston Library or a new fictional pilot. Establish the question, duration, participants, measured outcome, comparison, favorable result, limitation, cost or practical concern, unmeasured outcome, and next step. Separate measurements from evaluations. Use at least eight adjective-and-degree chunks that perform different jobs: one limited degree, one moderate degree, one substantial degree, one endpoint approach, two established collocations, one comparative modifier, and one <span class="ex">too</span> or <span class="ex">enough</span> consequence.</p><p>Include one classifying adjective, one adjective with a shifted reading, one <span class="ex">quite</span> phrase whose meaning you explain, and one active correction of an unsupported extreme claim. Deliver one sentence with default adjective prominence and another with contrastive intensifier prominence. Your listener changes the sample, measurement, criterion, comparison, cost, channel, or evidence quality; recalibrate the adjective and degree immediately.</p><p><b>Listener check:</b> Can the listener identify the underlying fact, scale, adjective reading, degree, collocation, criterion, register, evidence scope, comparison target, and consequence? Would removing the intensifier make the evaluation weaker, different, or simply more natural?</p></div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You preserved flexible adjective readings, conventional collocation, comparative grammar, too-and-enough limits, stance, spoken focus, and evidence-calibrated evaluation."><h3>Seven final decisions</h3><div class="quiz-list">
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Adjective types describe the current meaning and behavior; context can shift a word into a different reading." data-hint="Avoid treating the teaching categories as permanent properties of every word." data-fix="Classify the adjective's current reading, not the word forever."><p class="quiz-question">1. Which principle handles adjective type accurately?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Adjective types describe the current meaning and behavior; context can shift a word into a different reading.">Adjective types describe the current meaning and behavior; context can shift a word into a different reading.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Every adjective belongs to one permanent box and can never develop another reading.">Every adjective belongs to one permanent box and can never develop another reading.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Any intensifier combines naturally with every adjective in every genre.">Any intensifier combines naturally with every adjective in every genre.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Residents are deeply concerned, and the review is strongly opposed to removing the emergency exit." data-hint="Use deeply for profound concern and strongly for force of position." data-fix="Deeply concerned and strongly opposed preserve the two conventional meanings."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which sentence uses both collocations naturally?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Residents are deeply concerned, and the review is strongly opposed to removing the emergency exit.">Residents are deeply concerned, and the review is strongly opposed to removing the emergency exit.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Residents are strongly concerned, and the review is deeply opposed in every context.">Residents are strongly concerned, and the review is deeply opposed in every context.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Residents are perfectly concern, and the review highly oppose.">Residents are perfectly concern, and the review highly oppose.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The sample is large enough to test the procedure but too small to represent the national population." data-hint="Enough follows the adjective; too introduces the unmet broader purpose." data-fix="The same sample can meet one threshold and fail another when both purposes are named."><p class="quiz-question">3. Which sentence calibrates sufficiency against two different purposes?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The sample is large enough to test the procedure but too small to represent the national population.">The sample is large enough to test the procedure but too small to represent the national population.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The sample is enough large and very too small.">The sample is enough large and very too small.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The sample is twelve, so every possible purpose is completely proven.">The sample is twelve, so every possible purpose is completely proven.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The revision is considerably more reliable than the original in the 300-case staging test." data-hint="Use a comparative degree modifier and name the comparison scope." data-fix="Considerably modifies more reliable, and the staging test bounds the claim."><p class="quiz-question">4. Which comparison is grammatical and scoped?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The revision is considerably more reliable than the original in the 300-case staging test.">The revision is considerably more reliable than the original in the 300-case staging test.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The revision is very more reliable forever.">The revision is very more reliable forever.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The revision is most considerably reliable from nothing.">The revision is most considerably reliable from nothing.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Quite can express complete, extreme, or considerable degree; adjective, syntax, intonation, variety, and context determine the reading." data-hint="Do not assign quite one fixed percentage or one meaning across every adjective." data-fix="Quite has multiple degree readings that context resolves."><p class="quiz-question">5. Which account of quite is defensible?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Quite can express complete, extreme, or considerable degree; adjective, syntax, intonation, variety, and context determine the reading.">Quite can express complete, extreme, or considerable degree; adjective, syntax, intonation, variety, and context determine the reading.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Quite always means exactly 50 percent in every English variety.">Quite always means exactly 50 percent in every English variety.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Quite can modify only calendar nouns.">Quite can modify only calendar nouns.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The seven-decibel average difference is substantial, but the optional survey and missing comparison do not establish complete success." data-hint="Preserve the measured positive result and the broader evidence limits." data-fix="The adjective substantial fits the measured difference; complete success exceeds the record."><p class="quiz-question">6. Which Marston evaluation is calibrated?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The seven-decibel average difference is substantial, but the optional survey and missing comparison do not establish complete success.">The seven-decibel average difference is substantial, but the optional survey and missing comparison do not establish complete success.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Every outcome is absolutely perfect because 41 respondents reported easier focus.">Every outcome is absolutely perfect because 41 respondents reported easier focus.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Four warmth complaints prove that no user received any benefit.">Four warmth complaints prove that no user received any benefit.</button></div></div>
    <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Not FAIRly effective | HIGHly effective." data-hint="Put contrastive prominence on the rejected and replacement degrees." data-fix="The two prominent degree words correct moderate to high effectiveness."><p class="quiz-question">7. Which spoken delivery corrects a moderate evaluation to a high one?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Not FAIRly effective | HIGHly effective.">Not FAIRly effective | HIGHly effective.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="not fairly EFFECTIVE | highly EFFECTIVE with no audible degree contrast">not fairly EFFECTIVE | highly EFFECTIVE with no audible degree contrast</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Stress every syllable equally because degree never carries contrast.">Stress every syllable equally because degree never carries contrast.</button></div></div>
  </div><div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p></div>

  <h2>Reflect: what licenses this degree?</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card"><h3>Defend the evaluation, not only the combination</h3><p>Complete these lines aloud: <span class="ex">The observed fact is...</span> <span class="ex">My evaluation is...</span> <span class="ex">The relevant scale runs from...to...</span> <span class="ex">This adjective has a gradable / endpoint / extreme / classifying / participial reading because...</span> <span class="ex">The intensifier contributes...</span> <span class="ex">The established collocation is...</span> <span class="ex">The comparison target is...</span> <span class="ex">Too / enough is relative to...</span> <span class="ex">The register fits because...</span> <span class="ex">The evidence does not establish...</span></p><p>Then change the criterion or evidence. Recalibrate the adjective, intensifier, comparison, or consequence before changing the recommendation.</p><p><a href="#next-day-retrieval">Return tomorrow for the five-item retrieval check</a> before rereading the lesson.</p></div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Next use:</b> Find one nonprivate evaluation in a message or article. Record the fact, adjective reading, degree word, collocation, criterion, evidence, and a more cautious or stronger alternative.</div>
  <section id="next-day-retrieval" data-lesson-extension="Next-day adjective-reading, collocation, enough-position, comparative-modifier, and evidence-calibration retrieval"><h2>Next-day retrieval</h2><div class="lead-rule"></div><p>Return without rereading. Reconstruct five degree decisions in new fictional contexts.</p>
    <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You retained flexible adjective readings, conventional intensifier partnership, enough position, comparative modification, and evidence-calibrated evaluation."><h3>Five delayed decisions</h3><div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Weekly classifies literal frequency, while very 1990s uses a category label to mean highly characteristic of that style." data-hint="Contrast a literal category with a shifted resemblance reading." data-fix="A classifying word can gain a gradable qualitative reading when context explains the scale."><p class="quiz-question">1. Which explanation handles the two adjective readings accurately?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Weekly classifies literal frequency, while very 1990s uses a category label to mean highly characteristic of that style.">Weekly classifies literal frequency, while very 1990s uses a category label to mean highly characteristic of that style.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Very weekly and very 1990s both mean that a calendar contains extra weeks.">Very weekly and very 1990s both mean that a calendar contains extra weeks.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="No classifying word can ever be reinterpreted for typicality or resemblance.">No classifying word can ever be reinterpreted for typicality or resemblance.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The panel is deeply divided, but every member is strongly opposed to removing the only exit." data-hint="Use deeply for internal division and strongly for force of position." data-fix="Deeply divided and strongly opposed preserve both established collocations."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which sentence uses the two partnerships naturally?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The panel is deeply divided, but every member is strongly opposed to removing the only exit.">The panel is deeply divided, but every member is strongly opposed to removing the only exit.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The panel is highly divide, and each member deeply oppose.">The panel is highly divide, and each member deeply oppose.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The panel is perfectly opposition and bitter division.">The panel is perfectly opposition and bitter division.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The instructions are clear enough for a first-time user, and they provide enough examples for one practice round." data-hint="Place enough after the adjective but before the plural noun." data-fix="Use clear enough and enough examples."><p class="quiz-question">3. Which sentence places enough correctly in both patterns?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The instructions are clear enough for a first-time user, and they provide enough examples for one practice round.">The instructions are clear enough for a first-time user, and they provide enough examples for one practice round.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The instructions are enough clear and provide examples enough.">The instructions are enough clear and provide examples enough.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The instructions enough are clear and enough provide examples.">The instructions enough are clear and enough provide examples.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The revised route is slightly shorter but considerably more accessible than the original." data-hint="Use a small modifier before the first comparative and a large modifier before the second." data-fix="Slightly shorter and considerably more accessible distinguish the two difference sizes."><p class="quiz-question">4. Which comparison expresses one small and one large difference?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The revised route is slightly shorter but considerably more accessible than the original.">The revised route is slightly shorter but considerably more accessible than the original.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The revised route is very shorter but extreme more accessible.">The revised route is very shorter but extreme more accessible.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The revised route is absolute short and highly more accessible.">The revised route is absolute short and highly more accessible.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The pilot shows a promising pattern in the tested branches, but the voluntary sample and missing comparison prevent a conclusive general claim." data-hint="Keep the favorable direction, tested scope, and two evidence limits." data-fix="Promising but not conclusive matches a limited uncontrolled pilot."><p class="quiz-question">5. Which conclusion calibrates degree to the evidence?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The pilot shows a promising pattern in the tested branches, but the voluntary sample and missing comparison prevent a conclusive general claim.">The pilot shows a promising pattern in the tested branches, but the voluntary sample and missing comparison prevent a conclusive general claim.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The pilot is absolutely perfect proof for every branch and every user.">The pilot is absolutely perfect proof for every branch and every user.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The pilot contains no observations, participants, or possible value.">The pilot contains no observations, participants, or possible value.</button></div></div>
